well authorities in western germany are  rushing to clear mountains of debris and  restore  vital services after devastating floods  last week  many areas were left without access to  drinking water after the flood waters  damaged waste  water treatment plants and concerns are  now mounting over an environmental and  public health catastrophe  as sewage flows into rivers more rain is  forecast  complicating the cleanup  a main road into the flood hit area  covered in tons of rubble  most of it washed onto the road by the  floods and the rest dumped there by  clean-up workers  there's nowhere else to put it  towns are still surrounded by debris  and plagued by an overpowering stink  rivers are carrying rubbish and sewage  and the heat of summer is fueling fear  of disease  this is a sewage work serving one  flood-hit area  surrounded by a lake of filthy water  the works itself has been severely  damaged  it's unclear to the local authorities  when and if  it can be brought online again  until it can the waste water is left to  flow  right through it  at the moment we're working as fast as  we can to come up with a provisional  solution  the fact that the water is getting into  the river untreated  is an environmental catastrophe  the soil is contaminated with oil so of  course  there's been environmental damage but  our main priority is restoring the  infrastructure  for the people who live here  the regional government has set up an  emergency aid program to restore  wastewater plants  sewers and drinking water as fast as  possible  but at the moment it's still impossible  to estimate the total damage  one of the affected towns has already  received an emergency drinking water  system  but now the technicians have to move it  again  the weather forecast predicts rain for  this coming weekend  so the tanks are being moved to flat  ground higher up the workers  expect they'll be here for months  the new location has a lot of advantages  we've reactivated a well  so the quality of drinking water we were  supplying will be even better  and we can put the whole system indoors  more than a week after the floods hit  the river here is polluted  and washing the debris down towards the  rhine  well social media has played a key role  in connecting people affected by the  flooding with volunteers who want to  offer support  dw reporter tessa valtter visited one  town where a group of syrian refugees  answered  the call for help  with every shovel the piles of mud are  getting a little smaller  but after the enormous floods in west  germany there's still a lot left to do  here in zinter a group of young men is  helping the people worst affected by the  disaster  they came to germany as syrian refugees  six years ago  germany has given us a lot we've been  welcomed here  now we want to give something back  we want to help the people germans  foreigners  anyone hand in hand we are working  together  we're doing as much as we can  the young men answered the call on  social media  they've come from all over the country  and are paying their  travel expenses out of their own pockets  some are even using vacation days to be  here  anas al-akkad put out the call for  volunteers online  together with two friends he had a  simple but very clever idea  we collected pieces of information that  were already circulating on social media  in german  then we translated them so that arabic  speaking people could understand  once the information was out everybody  wanted to help  them ana's created a group online  hundreds have been signing up every day  i was very surprised when the numbers  started to go up  so quickly by now i'm a hundred percent  sure  that it could go up to a thousand  and when i imagine this place here with  thousands of people helping out  all together in an organized group that  really  gives me goosebumps  that kind of help is desperately needed  here  these home owners like many people in  the region  have lost almost everything  in these dire times the support given by  the young  man is highly appreciated  these guys really sacrificed themselves  for us africans syrians everybody  they worked all day for them it went  without saying  they've really helped a lot  [Applause]  with each day a little progress is made  for the volunteers there's still a lot  left to do  let's get the latest on the story now we  have our correspondent benjamin alvarez  gruber he is in availa one of the most  affected towns on the ground there for  us hi benjamin  how are people coping with these  challenges that we just heard about like  water supply and cleanup efforts  they're coping with these challenges  with the help of several organizations  that are here on the ground so we have  volunteers  for example installing mobile showers  and we also have the german red cross  that have installed  a clean water facility that you can see  behind me more than 26 have been  installed here  in this region more than a thousand  liters there's a lot of trucks  carrying this water around all day so  there's a big problem with water  and not just with drinking water there  are some people here in avala who got  water already but this is service water  and authorities are calling on people to  boil this water as  maybe also chemicals inside of this  water so people can just come here with  buckets with  bottles to fill up and to have some  water also  not just for the residents but also for  the volunteers so the german red cross  also wants to entitle mobile showers  as water and hygiene is a very delicate  topic for the authorities here as well  meanwhile authorities are also  scrambling benjamin to find people a lot  of people are still missing so  how was the search for them going  the search continues rescue teams think  that they might continue  finding bodies as they still are pumping  water out of houses and of underground  parking lots  many of them don't think that they will  be still able to find survivors only  here there more than 155  missing there is the german army the  bundesver soldiers  police officers and firefighters who are  working around the clock to still try to  assess and  still try to reach houses so the  volunteers are also playing a very  important role here  we've seen a lot of groups of volunteers  have made it  to this place their shuttles also with  buses to prevent also  problems with traffic is ambulances and  firefighters need to get quickly  to the scene so there are a lot of  people working here on the ground the  search continues but  unfortunately the hope to find survivors  is it  continues to decrease in addition to all  of these challenges  more rain is forecast for this coming  weekend  how will that affect the search and  cleanup efforts  that's right the german meteorological  service is expecting heavy rains on  saturday and sunday there are several  groups preparing in case  more evacuations are needed and  several organizations are asking for  more help to see how this  where these people could live there are  already several emergency shelters here  but of course they might need to  evacuate  even more people so they try to find a  solution  ahead of the work the rescue work that  of course if the rain is too strong on  saturday and sunday will not be able  to continue with that so authorities are  preparing for the worse even though the  numbers and the focus says  that it will not be as heavy as the rain  floods  that we saw last week our correspondent  benjamin alvarez gruber in availa  thank you for that update
 

